-- title:
--   Coefficient obstruction using quadratic-pair recurrences
-- statement:
--   Retain the parent's geometric, polynomial, contact-ideal, scalar formal-flow, quadratic-reduction and numerical hypotheses. At a point r write yᵣ=Jᵣ(2), Dᵣ=4Pᵣ³−g₂Pᵣ−g₃, Aₚᵣ=ρ(p)(J₀ᵣ), and Bₚᵣ=(∂₂ρ(p))(J₀ᵣ), where J₀ replaces the wp-prime coordinate by zero. The parent's Weierstrass identity says yᵣ²=Dᵣ.
--
--   Define Tₚᵣ(i)=(uₚᵣᵢ,vₚᵣᵢ) by the fixed recurrence (u₀,v₀)=(1,0), uᵢ₊₁=Aₚᵣuᵢ+DᵣBₚᵣvᵢ, vᵢ₊₁=Bₚᵣuᵢ+Aₚᵣvᵢ. Assume the norm certificate uₚᵣᵢ²−Dᵣvₚᵣᵢ²=(Aₚᵣ²−DᵣBₚᵣ²)ⁱ for every p,r,i. The completed theorem supplies this certificate from the existing Weierstrass identity.
--
--   The task is to choose the parent's witnesses p,F,a,b,s and exclude the same finite coefficient-interpolation weights, using uₚᵣᵢ+yᵣvₚᵣᵢ in place of (Aₚᵣ+yᵣBₚᵣ)ⁱ. The contact-quotient relation and all numerical bounds remain the same. A kernel-checked converse establishes the reverse implication with identical witnesses and weights. The geometric exclusion of the resulting system remains open.
-- source:
--   Derived quadratic-pair recurrence step for https://prove2.me/theorems/331d9140-bf3f-4a7e-98d5-ccf5729c2b74. Mission context: Senthil Kumar K (2026), Algebraic independence of values of Weierstrass elliptic and zeta functions, Appendix A.2, Theorem A.3 and Proposition A.1; https://doi.org/10.1017/S001309152610145X. This is an algebraic tool for the interpolation frontier; the geometric zero estimate remains open. Primary Lean sources: Mathlib Algebra/QuadraticAlgebra/Defs.lean and Basic.lean, especially the universal lift and multiplicative norm, revision 0df444a360eaa60ab8c11dca51a86af692955474. The recurrence is multiplication in the quadratic algebra with generator squared equal to d. Its evaluation homomorphism reconstructs powers, and its multiplicative norm proves the norm identity. The frontier uses both identities with d equal to the Weierstrass cubic evaluated at the formal wp series. Both directions preserve all witnesses, weights and numerical bounds.

